d very  intentionally and that was the phrase  all things being equal because if all  things were equal that argument be  completely valid in strict fairness that  should be no different but the thing is  all things aren't equal that's not the  world that we live in and the vast  majority of major roles in blockbuster  films go  white actors disproportionately so so  we're not starting from an even playing  field and that argument that well if you  cast a white character as black or  anything else and that's fine but this  isn't bad argument only works if we're  on a level playing field or something  close to it and we're just not so we're  going to set that aside and move on so  the question now becomes is it ever  appropriate to cast a traditionally  ethnic whether it be Asian or black or  whatever character with a white actor  for the film version and there are many  people who make the argument no it is  never appropriate I actually don't quite  land there even though I recognize the  the unfairness in terms of what roles  are going disproportionately to what  group of actors who look a certain way  because there's a certain amount of  context that you need to give certain  interpretations that I think just the  black and white no you can never do it  just overlooks and I'm going to cite a  couple specific examples and I'm going  to start with the departed now this was  the movie that Martin Scorsese finally  won his directing Oscar for now what you  may or may not know is that this was  actually a remake the original was a  Japanese film called infernal affairs  now that original film was set in Tokyo  and it dealt with the Yakuza but it was  that same basic plot of the Yakuza have  a mole in the police department the  police department have them all in the  Yakuza and because it's set in Tokyo  dealing with the our Kuzon it's very  steeped in Japanese culture and history  and all the rest of it now when Martin  Scorsese and his team made their version  it was 