 so it's looking  like they're doing that approach that I  talked about they're taking it and  moving it somewhere else and changing  the setting and that allows them for  this free casting so that should end the  argument right well here's the rub let's  look at this picture again that look is  the look very specifically of Motor Co  Kusanagi that color palette is the color  palette of the classic 90s anime film  version of this story this film isn't  just taking the bare-bones concept  Andrea propriate in it into something  new it's treading on the iconography and  it's very much absorbing all of the  things from earlier versions and from  the original manga and its end it Keith  it's keeping the name its banking on all  of this stuff that is originating from  its Japanese source material and in  doing so it kind of loses credibility in  terms of that while we're moving into  another culture argument they're not  changing it to represent a different  culture the way that the departed did or  edge of tomorrow did and that  muddies the waters a little bit now  where does that mean this lands  ultimately honestly i would say kind of  in the middle what i mean when i say  that is this is ultimately an example of  what many are calling whitewashing i  feel that term is a little overly harsh  in the specific instance but it does  serve as an example of that you're  taking a Japanese character and  maintaining the Japanese story and even  some of the Japanese supporting  characters around her but you've changed  that to accommodate a white actor but at  the same time it is maintaining a very  culturally diverse cast and given a lot  of the elements of the story in terms of  cybernetic bodies and everything else  there is a narrative argument to be made  that could I'm not going to say  necessarily prove this casting correct  but you can justify it what I say just  if I mean from a strictly narrative  perspective because if you're going to  look at it from a cultural stance not  it's much harder to justify in it is a  problem and I think that's where people  rightly have a certain amount of anger  because this is yet another example of  something that has been an ongoing issue  the thing is that people are getting  have gotten very hung up on this  specific example is it an example of  whitewashing and casting yes honestly it  is is it the strongest example of it  well we're having this discussion in the  same year in which a movie called gods  of Egypt start an Australian a dane and  a Scotsman so no this is not the best  example but it is another example to  illustrate an overall pattern a systemic  issue in which actors of non-white  ethnicities are simply not given the  opportunity to grow and become stars and  then it becomes a self-perpetuating  cycle because Hollywood says well we  have to cast Scarlett Johansson because  she's a box-office strong there's no big  name Asian female box-office drawers but  they're never giving them the rules to  let them become box-office drawers and  it just goes round and round around so I  think what we have to a certain extent  in how big the conversation has gotten  around a specific movie is a little bit  of case of missing the forest for the  trees it is absolutely an example and  it's one more instance 2.2 along with  things like gods of Egypt or Exodus or  any of  other examples but the what gives it  weight is the number of instances this  specific instance I don't actually think  makes the best case add it to the pile  certainly but you need to make the  bigger case as opposed to get hung up  into the arguments as to whether or not  this specific instance is the most  egregious I think that's where  unfortunately conversation has got  installed what should have happened is  the casting of Scarlett Johansson should  have kick-started a bigger conversation  but unfortunately what's happened  instead is the people have said this is  an example of what we're talking about  and other people will come back no it's  not it's fine for them to do this  because of X and then the first site has  to refute that argument and then it gets  refuted again and we're just we're never  moving past this one movie and when we  can't move past the specific example  that's when the problem just never gets  fixed and the one thing that I think is  worth just mentioning at the end of this  is Japan itself by and large doesn't  seem to care because their reaction was  kind of what I said at the beginning  which was Hollywood's making it we  always assumed it was gonna be a white  person cast now does the fact that the  country of origin is not outraged mean  that nobody should be outraging you have  no right to your outrage no of course  not because the outrage isn't really  about this specific movie it's about the  Hollywood System and systemic issues  that are built in to who gets an  opportunity to lead a movie and who  doesn't and it's an American problem  it's not a problem with this specific  film although this specific film can be  used as an example of the bigger issue  so ultimately is this a bit of a cop-out  answer huh maybe it is so if you are  want to call me out as just towing the  middle line and refusing to take a stand  out you know what you tell me what your  stance is whatever it is drop something  down in the comments and let's talk  about it let's try and have a civil  discussion please that's what I hope to  get out of all this I'm trying to weigh  all sides and keep it civil so I mean  whatever your feelings are just be  respectful so let's have a nice  conversation you can follow me on  twitter at council of geeks check out  the council of geeks podcast at stitcher  and on iTunes you can email me council  geeks @ gmail.com and until next time  this council is  earned
 

